														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1544565">
									

<p>There's also the fact that there would be certain people who'd be immune to self-contradictory statements. Namely, mathematical logicians. It's fairly well accepted among logicians that self-contradictory statements actually hold a &quot;null&quot; truth value in the absence of an acceptable outside framework to firmly define &quot;truth/falsehood&quot;. In such a framework, that statement literally couldn't exist: all things would be either wholly true, wholly false, conditionally true, or conditionally false. Self-contradiction wouldn't be possible.</p>
<p>So yeah, just like someone could adjust to &quot;open&quot; a &quot;closed&quot; door, a properly trained logician should be able to (eventually) rationalize away that statement, even in the face of the ring's effect.</p>

