														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1544711">
									

<p>Possibly I overstate the case, but the tone slips are numerous. I'll point out the first couple that jump at me and come back when I have more time for a more thorough list.</p>
<p>-Redacting the approving Docs name in the containment, while not precisely a tone issue, is the first mis-step I see.<br />
-&quot;Only non-destructive testing&#8230;&quot; That should be part of the containment, as it deals with what one can/can't do with the item.<br />
-&quot;wholeheartedly&quot;, &quot;completely unshakeable &quot;, &quot;<strong>simply</strong> accepting&quot;, &quot;by far&quot; are all less than clinical word choices. Most of the slips were of that nature. I'll get you a more thorough list in that later pass.<br />
-Pretty much all the bits in parentheses read like conversational asides rather than scientific notes.</p>
<p>Now, having defended my inner copy-editor I will shut him in his little cell and lock the door, because if he heard what I am about to say he would ravage my medula. I don't neccesarily recomend fixing these. Good writing overrides poor tone, and you have some very good writing here IMO. If you can patch it up without losing the sparkle, hooray! But don't take chances on spoiling a good thing over some nitpicks.</p>

							</div>

								
					
